cpp - the standalone C preprocessor

cmd/cpp is /bin/cpp, the preprocessor the distribution shipped.  It is
John F. Reiser's cpp from v7 (usr/src/cmd/cpp), the one the v7 README
calls "5 to 12 times faster than the old", ported to micronix.

NOT THE COMPILER

This program is a filter: standard input to standard output.  It is
NOT a pass of the C compiler, and it has nothing to do with the
compiler's front end, which is /libexec/pass0.

The two programs share a name only because the compiler's front end
inherited the name "cpp" from the code it grew out of.  cmd/TODO tells
the story: pass0 (whose sources are libexec/pass0/cpp.c and cpp.h) is
the lexer and normalizer that emits the compact-token ".x" stream the
parser reads, and the name is wrong - ccc runs it as pass0 and builds
that name into its path.  This cpp, /bin/cpp, is the real thing: an
ordinary standalone preprocessor of the filter kind, and the gap that
used to sit in /bin until it was ported.

Do not confuse the two.  If you are touching the compiler, its front
end is under libexec/pass0.  If you are touching the preprocessor the
shell can run, it is here.

WHAT WAS CHANGED

The source is Reiser's, nearly verbatim.  The differences are all
compile-time machine choices resolved for micronix, and none change
behaviour:

  - COFF is 128, not 0.  ccc's plain char is SIGNED (c0/eutil.c says
    so), so a byte above 0x7f scans as a negative number and fastab,
    toktyp, slotab and macbit are indexed with a +128 offset - the
    same reason Reiser wrote COFF 128 for pdp11 and vax.

  - The gcos, ibm, tgp, gimpel, interdata, tss, os and mert
    conditionals are resolved away.  The system is unix-shaped: the
    standard include directory is /usr/include, a bare directory name
    is ".", and an output file after an input file is honoured.

  - scw1 is on, scw2 is off.  The single-character macro-name test
    stays; the adjacent-pair test and its tables are gone.

  - The one predefined symbol is z80, as "1".  It matches the
    compiler: ccc's own front end predefines z80=1, so `cpp` and `ccc`
    agree on `#if z80`.  The original predefined `unix`; that was
    dropped so the two do not disagree.

  - cpy.y is not run through yacc.  The cross build has no yacc to run
    before this directory builds, so cpy.c is the same grammar written
    by hand, one function per precedence level.  cpy.y and yylex.c are
    kept as the source of the source; yylex.c is #included by cpy.c
    exactly as cpy.y's `# include "yylex.c"` did.

  - The two-file case (input file then output file) opens the output
    plainly instead of reusing stdout's buffer through setbuf and the
    v7 _sobuf, which micronix's stdio does not have.

FLAGS

    cpp [ -PRC ] [ -Dname[=val] ] [ -Uname ] [ -Idir ] [ in [ out ] ]

  -Dname[=val]  define name as 1, or as val
  -Uname        remove an initial definition
  -Idir         add dir to the include search path (before /usr/include)
  -P            do not emit "# line file" control lines
  -R            allow macro recursion (normally a recursion is an error)
  -C            pass comments through instead of deleting them

Symbols defined with -D are "1" unless a value is given.  A missing
-D argument is ignored.  The search order for `#include "file"` is the
including file's own directory, then -I directories, then
/usr/include; for `#include <file>` it is the -I directories, then
/usr/include.

LICENSE

The v7 sources in extra/ (and the ports of them here, like cal, tty and
the rest) are Bell Labs Unix released under Caldera's license.  The
ATTRIBUTIONS.md at the top of the tree records this cpp as a v7 port.
